Vanilla Conquer: Red Alert for RISC OS
======================================

Preview release

Command & Conquer: Red Alert (1996) for the Risc PC and the Raspberry Pi,
built from the Vanilla Conquer source code, which is based on the game's
source code as released by Electronic Arts under the GNU GPL.

Source code, updates and bug reports:
  https://github.com/davidpsharp/riscos-vanilla-conquer

The title screen shows the version and when it was built.


Requirements
------------

  * A StrongARM Risc PC (or RPCEmu set up as one), or a Raspberry Pi
    with RISC OS 5. The same !VanillaRA runs on both. ARM610, ARM710 and
    ARM7500 machines (including the A7000) are not supported.
  * RISC OS 3.7 or later. Tested on RISC OS 4.39 on a real StrongARM
    Risc PC, RISC OS 3.71 in RPCEmu and RISC OS 5.30 on a Raspberry Pi 4.
  * A 640x400 (or 640x480) 256-colour screen mode, or 320x256 for the
    low-resolution option below.
  * About 40 MB of free memory. Most of it is taken by a dynamic area
    called "Vanilla RA heap", which goes when the game quits.
  * About 110 MB of disc space for the game data without movies, or
    480 MB (Allied) or 525 MB (Soviet) for one disc's data with its
    movies.
  * For sound, the DigitalRenderer module (included). Without it the
    game runs silently.

SharedUnixLibrary and DigitalRenderer are included in !VanillaRA.Modules.
!Run uses newer copies from !System if you have them.

On a Risc PC the first campaign missions play at full speed (the others
haven't been timed yet). Big battles with over a hundred units on screen
slow the game down; the low-resolution option helps.


Installing the game data
------------------------

The game data isn't included. You need the freeware Red Alert CD images
that Electronic Arts released in 2008: the Allied disc and/or the Soviet
disc. Make sure you get the Windows 95 versions. The expansion packs
(Counterstrike and Aftermath) aren't supported yet.

As of October 2026 they can be downloaded from cnc-comm.com:

  Allied disc:  https://cnc-comm.com/red-alert/downloads/the-game/allies-disc
  Soviet disc:  https://cnc-comm.com/red-alert/downloads/the-game/soviet-disc

The downloads are zip files (RA_Allies.zip and RA_Soviet.zip), each
holding one .iso file of about 600 MB (CD1_ALLIES.iso and CD2_SOVIET.iso).
Unzip them first; that's easiest on a modern computer. These images have
been checked, and every file matched.

To install them:

  1. Put !VanillaRA where you want to keep the game, and double-click
     it. Its icon appears on the icon bar.
  2. Drag the ISO file(s) onto that icon: both discs at once, or one
     after the other.

When you drop a disc, the game says which one it is, how much space its
game data and its movies (370 MB on the Allied disc, 415 MB on the Soviet one) need, and how much is free,
and asks whether to install the game data only or the movies too.
Without the movies the game simply skips them; drop the disc again later
to add them. If there isn't room for the movies as well, it says how much
more space they need; if there isn't room even for the game data, it says
so and installs nothing.

The game copies the data into the gamedata directory inside !VanillaRA,
so the ISO files can be on any disc, and can be deleted afterwards. It
reads them directly: no CD drive or CDFS is needed. Meanwhile a window
shows which file it's copying and how far it has got, and the desktop
carries on as usual; each disc takes a few minutes. When it has finished,
the window says which discs are installed and which you can still add;
click the icon to play.

Deleting the gamedata directory uninstalls the game data: the game then
asks for the CD images again. Your settings and saved games are kept
elsewhere, in Choices. Data installed by an earlier version, straight
into !VanillaRA, is moved into gamedata the next time the game starts.

Each file is checked against a known checksum as it's copied. If
anything goes wrong, a window lists what happened. A file that comes out
wrong, or a copy that is stopped part-way (a full disc, a power cut),
leaves nothing that looks installed: drag the image onto the icon again
to finish. Files already installed are skipped, so that's always safe.

If installing goes wrong, the file InstallLog in the gamedata directory
records what happened; to see it, Shift-double-click !VanillaRA to open
the application. Please include it when you report the problem.

The game recognises each disc by its contents, and says so if it's the
wrong one: a C&C95 or Tiberian Sun disc, for instance, goes on the
!VanillaTD or !OpenTS icon. It spots a download that didn't finish, too.

Each time the game starts, it checks that its files are all there at the
right sizes (it takes a moment). Until they are, clicking the icon says
what's missing, instead of starting the game. Help on the icon's menu
opens this file.
*Set VanillaRA$NoDataCheck 1 skips that check, for game data from
elsewhere.

With the movies, each disc's data goes in its own folder
(gamedata.allied and gamedata.soviet), and the game uses whichever it
needs, as it would the CDs. Without them, one 85 MB copy of the game data
is written instead, the same from either disc, so one disc is then enough
for both campaigns.

From the command line, Utils.vcprep installs from images too, with
-nomovies to leave out the movies and -force to rewrite files already
there:

  *WimpSlot -min 6000K -max 6000K
  *Run <VanillaRA$Dir>.Utils.vcprep -raapp <VanillaRA$Dir> SCSI::Big.$.CD1_ALLIES/iso


Playing
-------

Double-click !VanillaRA to put its icon on the icon bar, then click the
icon to start the game. The keys are the same as in the original game.
Shift+F12 in the game goes back to the desktop, with the game paused
where it was (sound too); click the icon again to carry on. The icon's
menu has Info, Help (this file), Screen size (see below) and Quit.

Settings are in !VanillaRA.INI.REDALERT, which the game creates when you
change its options.

Screen size: the first time it runs, the game measures this machine's
speed for under a second (the desktop pauses) and picks the largest
screen in the shape of the desktop that it can keep smooth: the
desktop's own size on a Raspberry Pi 4, 640x400 on a Risc PC. Bigger
screens show more of the battlefield, with a taller sidebar; the menus,
movies and briefings stay 640x400, in the middle of the screen.

To choose another size, use Screen size on the icon's menu: Automatic
(the size it picked, shown in brackets), the usual sizes the monitor
can show, and 320x200 (low resolution). Choose it before clicking the
icon to start, and the game starts at that size. The choice is saved
for next time. Chosen while a game is in progress (after Shift+F12), it
restarts the game, after asking: the game in progress is lost, so save
it first. Any size from 640x400 up to 2048x2048 can also be set in
!VanillaRA.INI.REDALERT (a text file), for example:

  [Video]
  Resolution=1024x768

Low resolution: for slower machines, the game can run at 320x200, the
size of the original DOS version, which is about twice as fast on a Risc
PC. It uses the game's own low-resolution graphics: less of the map
fits on the screen and the sidebar is smaller.

Sound alongside other programs: DigitalRenderer plays sound for one
program at a time. To mix the game's sound with other programs' instead,
set VanillaRA$Sound to SharedSoundBuffer in !VanillaRA.!Run. That needs
SharedSound 1.07 or later (part of RISC OS 5), StreamManager and
SharedSoundBuffer in !System. StreamManager and SharedSoundBuffer are John
Duffell's freeware and aren't included; they are in ssb.zip on the
RDPClient page, http://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html.
A program that plays through DigitalRenderer still takes the sound for
itself while it plays. Without the modules, DigitalRenderer plays the
game's sound as usual.

Saved games are stored in !VanillaRA. They can be moved between RISC OS
machines but can't be loaded by Vanilla Conquer on other platforms, or
by the original game.


Network play
------------

Choose Multiplayer Game, then Network. Players on the same local network
see each other's games. A Risc PC and a Raspberry Pi can play each other,
each at its own screen size; this has been tried with a 45-minute game
with computer players between a Pi 3 and a Risc PC.

  * All the machines must be on the same subnet.
  * Wired Ethernet is recommended: some wireless networks drop the
    broadcast messages that the game uses to find other players.
  * If the machines still can't see each other, set VC_NETPEER to the
    other players' IP addresses before starting the game, e.g.
      *Set VC_NETPEER 192.168.1.20,192.168.1.21


Known issues
------------

  * Campaign missions have only been tried in part: Allied and Soviet
    missions 1 to 7 run on the Risc PC and the Pi 4, and Allied 1 has been
    started from the menus and played a little on the Risc PC.


Problems
--------

Messages from the game go to !VanillaRA.stderr. If something goes wrong,
please include that file in a bug report. To also log frame rates and
timings there:

  *Set VC_FPSLOG 1


